RESP {"id":3,"jsonrpc":"2.0","result":{"content":[{"text":"{\"backend\":\"acos-native\",\"capabilities_used\":[],\"fidelity\":\"static-analysis\",\"metrics\":{\"wall_ms\":0},\"ok\":true,\"request_id\":\"req_help\",\"result\":{\"available_command_fidelity\":[{\"fidelity\":\"static-analysis\",\"tool\":\"acos_inspect\"},{\"fidelity\":\"native-runtime\",\"tool\":\"acos_run_dex\"},{\"fidelity\":\"native-runtime\",\"tool\":\"acos_run\"},{\"fidelity\":\"native-runtime\",\"tool\":\"acos_observe\"},{\"fidelity\":\"native-runtime\",\"tool\":\"acos_action\"},{\"fidelity\":\"native-runtime\",\"tool\":\"acos_reset\"},{\"fidelity\":\"native-runtime\",\"tool\":\"acos_destroy\"},{\"fidelity\":\"native-runtime\",\"tool\":\"acos_verify\"}],\"backend\":{\"binary\":\"/home/Qui/.t3/worktrees/acos/t3-9fde79f7-5b90a224f291fea1/.cache/rg1-prefix/share/acos/0.3.0/bin/acos\",\"probe_error\":null,\"probed\":true,\"version\":\"acos 0.3.0\"},\"compatibility\":null,\"default_command_fidelity\":\"static-analysis\",\"error_playbook\":[{\"action\":\"Do not rewrite the app to hide the gap. Read details, try another fidelity/target, or report unsupported. Never a pass.\",\"category\":\"engine cannot do it (status=unsupported)\",\"codes\":[\"UNSUPPORTED_ANDROID_API\",\"UNSUPPORTED_JAVA_API\",\"UNSUPPORTED_BYTECODE\",\"UNSUPPORTED_NATIVE_SYMBOL\",\"UNSUPPORTED_RELOCATION\",\"NEEDS_REAL_DEVICE\",\"REQUIRES_GMS\",\"WRONG_ABI\",\"NO_DISPLAY\",\"NO_TEXT\",\"UNSUPPORTED_BACKEND\",\"RENDER_UNSUPPORTED_OP\"]},{\"action\":\"Read the assertion diff (expected/actual/path) and the linked frame/tree, then fix the app or the expectation and rerun.\",\"category\":\"the app is wrong (status=fail)\",\"codes\":[\"ASSERTION_FAILED\",\"ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND\"]},{\"action\":\"Read the crash/ANR event and last actions; fix the app or the unsupported platform call. Never a pass.\",\"category\":\"app crashed or hung\",\"codes\":[\"APP_CRASHED\",\"APP_NOT_RESPONDING\",\"JAVA_EXCEPTION\"]},{\"action\":\"Rebuild/re-sign/re-package and retry inspect first; these are artifact problems, not app verdicts.\",\"category\":\"bad build artifact\",\"codes\":[\"APK_NOT_ZIP\",\"APK_NO_MANIFEST\",\"APK_IO\",\"APK_ENTRY\",\"APK_SIGNING_MALFORMED\",\"AXML_MALFORMED\",\"ARSC_MALFORMED\",\"DEX_BAD_*\",\"DEX_TRUNCATED\",\"DEX_VERIFY_FAILED\",\"DEX_LOAD_ERROR\",\"ELF_MALFORMED\",\"NATIVE_DEP_NOT_FOUND\",\"NATIVE_INIT_FAILED\",\"NATIVE_SYMBOL_NOT_FOUND\"]},{\"action\":\"Create the session first, serialise mutations, re-observe after mutations, reduce concurrency or raise an explicit budget.\",\"category\":\"session / resource problems\",\"codes\":[\"SESSION_NOT_FOUND\",\"SESSION_BUSY\",\"STALE_REF\",\"TIMEOUT\",\"RESOURCE_LIMIT\",\"RAM_GUARD\",\"RAM_ABORT\"]},{\"action\":\"Wait for a new frame, use after_seq/after_cause, re-run at a qualifying fidelity, and write artifacts inside the artifacts directory.\",\"category\":\"evidence / fidelity problems\",\"codes\":[\"NO_FRESH_FRAME\",\"UNVERIFIED_FIDELITY\",\"ARTIFACTS_INVALID\",\"UNSAFE_PATH\"]},{\"action\":\"Fix every reported path/field; choose an isolation level the engine supports or report the missing guarantee.\",\"category\":\"invalid input\",\"codes\":[\"INVALID_SCENARIO\",\"INVALID_ARGUMENT\",\"ISOLATION_UNAVAILABLE\",\"ISOLATION_UNSATISFIED\"]},{\"action\":\"An ACOS bug or strict-rendering rejection. Capture request/scenario/events; do not retry blindly or claim pass.\",\"category\":\"harness failure\",\"codes\":[\"VM_INTERNAL\",\"INTERNAL\",\"RENDER_*\",\"RENDER_STRICT\"]}],\"fidelity_rules\":[{\"level\":\"static-analysis\",\"proves\":\"host APK/DEX parsing only; no runtime behaviour\"},{\"level\":\"jvm-reference\",\"proves\":\"host OpenJDK pure-Java semantics only; never Android/ART/framework/JNI/rendering\"},{\"level\":\"native-runtime\",\"proves\":\"ACOS executed it; not ART-validated unless validated_against says so\"},{\"level\":\"art-reference\",\"proves\":\"real ART on a reference image; authoritative for the stated API/ABI\"},{\"level\":\"physical-device\",\"proves\":\"a real device/OEM target\"},{\"level\":\"simulated\",\"proves\":\"the mock path only; not an Android success\"},{\"level\":\"n/a\",\"proves\":\"nothing about app behaviour: this is a meta reply (help/capabilities/doctor) about ACOS itself\"}],\"guide\":\"## Recipes (answer these first)\\n\\n**Take a screenshot.** There is no capture tool on the MCP surface yet. Two\\nroutes work today: (a) a `capture: {name: shot, format: png, fresh: true}` step\\nin an `acos_verify` scenario — the PNG lands as `frames/<seq>.png` under the\\nartifacts directory and the step JSON names it; (b) with the CLI on a live\\ndaemon session, `acos observe --screenshot out/shot.png --json`. Coming:\\n`screenshot: true` on `acos_observe_tree`, which returns the PNG path in the\\nreply (and `NO_FRESH_FRAME` instead of a silent no-op when the frame carries no\\npixels).\\n\\n**Type into a field.** Observe the tree, tap the field (`acos_tap {\\\"id\\\": ...}`\\nor `{\\\"text\\\": ...}`), type with `acos_text {\\\"text\\\": \\\"Ada\\\"}`, then observe again to\\nread the value. Text goes to the *focused* editor through the real\\n`InputConnection`; `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND` with `details.kind = NoFocusedEditor`\\nmeans the tap did not land on an editable field. If `acos_text` answers\\n`UNSUPPORTED_BACKEND` on a live session, the same interaction as a scenario\\n`type:` step runs through `acos_verify` and does work.\\n\\n**Find a control by `content_desc`.** `acos_observe_tree` returns `text` **and**\\n`content_desc` per node; icon buttons usually carry their name only in\\n`content_desc` (for example `Search Japanese dictionary`). Select it in a\\nscenario with `selector: {content_desc: {contains: Search}}`, or hand the label\\nto `acos_tap {\\\"text\\\": ...}` (text or content-desc), or tap the centre of the\\nnode's `bounds` with `x`/`y`. Never read a coordinate off a PNG: PNG space is\\noffset by `frame.display_origin`.\\n\\n**Prove a flow.** Put the interaction in a YAML scenario (`topic: \\\"scenarios\\\"`)\\nand run `acos_verify` (real ART by default). The pass counts only with its\\nevidence on disk: `verdict.json`, `steps/NN-*.json` and the referenced frames —\\nand `expect_change: true` on the taps whose point is to change the screen is\\nwhat makes the pass mean something.\\n\\n**Launch arguments.** Paths must be **absolute**: the server's cwd is not your\\nshell's, and a relative path is `INVALID_ARGUMENT` with\\n`details.resolved_path`. Each launch rung carries `ms`. Pass `\\\"label\\\": \\\"...\\\"`\\nand, if you will park the session, `\\\"idle_ttl_s\\\": N`: the server destroys its\\nown sessions after the idle TTL (default 600 s, `ACOS_SESSION_IDLE_TTL_S`) and\\nthe next call on an expired one returns `SESSION_NOT_FOUND` with\\n`details.reason=idle_ttl_expired` and the TTL.\\n\\n**Assert, do not eyeball.** `assert_text` (optionally with `bounds`),\\n`assert_visible` / `assert_not_visible` (collapsed content stays in the tree with\\n`visible: false`, so assert visibility, never presence) and `assert_log` turn a\\nvisual claim into a machine check with a diff.\\nA step settles on the screen its action produced; a change the app makes **later** (a delayed post, a worker thread, a timer) may land after the step settled. Check such an effect with `assert_text` + `timeout_ms` (a real wait), not with the action step's result.\\n\\n## The loop\\n\\n```\\ndoctor -> inspect -> launch -> observe_tree -> tap/text/key/swipe -> logs -> verify -> reset -> destroy\\n```\\n\\n1. **Preflight the host.** `acos doctor --json` (CLI) or `acos_doctor` (MCP).\\n   It checks the Android SDK, `d8`, `android.jar`, the JDK, the workspace\\n   helper, the signing keystore, the boot-classpath cache and host memory, and\\n   every failing check carries a `fix:` line. Fix those first: without them a\\n   later failure is a host problem wearing an app's clothes.\\n2. **Know the APK before running it.** `acos_inspect` / `acos inspect <apk>`\\n   is static only (package, launcher activity, min/target SDK, DEX and native\\n   inventory). It never executes the app and is never proof of runtime\\n   behaviour. It also cross-checks the **native libraries the app asks for**\\n   against the ones the APK ships: `result.native_lib_check.warnings` lists\\n   `{\\\"code\\\": \\\"NATIVE_LIB_MISSING\\\", \\\"lib\\\": \\\"libworklets.so\\\", \\\"abi\\\": \\\"x86_64\\\",\\n   \\\"source\\\": \\\"elf-needed\\\"|\\\"dex-load-library\\\", \\\"referenced_by\\\": [...]}` for every\\n   library named by a bundled library's `DT_NEEDED` or by a\\n   `System.loadLibrary`/`SoLoader.loadLibrary` call site in the DEX that has no\\n   `lib/<abi>/` entry. Platform libraries (`libc.so`, `libm.so`, …) are never\\n   warnings, and a healthy APK answers `warnings: []` — so a non-empty list is a\\n   **hint**, not the cause: it says \\\"this library is referenced and absent\\\", not\\n   \\\"this is why the app misbehaves\\\". Treat it as the first thing to check before\\n   debugging the UI, then confirm at runtime.\\n3. **Run it.** `acos_launch {\\\"apk\\\": \\\"...\\\"}` (MCP, session persists) or\\n   `acos launch <apk> --engine art --json`. **The MCP session tools always run\\n   real ART** (the server starts its own private `acos serve --engine art`\\n   daemon); the `acos` CLI's own default route is the frozen Rust VM, so pass\\n   `--engine art` there. Read the ladder:\\n   `apk_parsed -> process_booted -> bind_application -> application_on_create ->\\n   client_transaction -> activity_on_create -> on_resume -> first_frame`.\\n   `reached_stage` is the honest answer to \\\"how far did it get\\\"; the first\\n   rung that is not `ok` carries the blocker. **A launch that gets a window but\\n   never mounts the app's own content is `blocked` with\\n   `error.code = PLACEHOLDER_CONTENT`**, never `pass`/`first_frame`: the reply's\\n   `result.content` says `{\\\"mounted\\\": false, \\\"reason\\\": \\\"placeholder-content\\\"}`,\\n   `stage_reached` is `window`, and `error.details.log_hint` quotes the one guest\\n   log line that explains it (an `UnsatisfiedLinkError`, a `SoLoader` line, a\\n   `couldn't find` line, else the first `E` line; if the log says nothing the\\n   hint says so instead of guessing). The usual cause is a native library the\\n   APK references and does not ship — `acos_inspect`'s\\n   `native_lib_check.warnings` names it statically. Do not retry the same APK\\n   with a longer timeout; it is the packaging.\\n4. **Observe before acting.** `acos_observe_tree` returns nodes with\\n   `resource_id`, `text`, `content_desc`, `class`, `bounds`,\\n   `clickable/enabled/focused/scrollable`. Use those bounds and ids for the\\n   next step. Before the app has a window the reply is `unsupported` with\\n   `details.reason = \\\"not_ready\\\"` — never an empty tree.\\n5. **Act.** `acos_tap {\\\"text\\\": \\\"Increment\\\"}` (preferred),\\n   `acos_tap {\\\"content_desc\\\": \\\"Search Japanese dictionary\\\"}`,\\n   `acos_tap {\\\"id\\\": \\\"inc\\\"}`, `acos_tap {\\\"x\\\":.., \\\"y\\\":..}`,\\n   `acos_key {\\\"code\\\": \\\"back\\\"}`, `acos_text {\\\"text\\\": \\\"hello\\\"}` (types into the\\n   focused editor — the same guest `type_text` command a scenario `type` step\\n   sends), `acos_swipe {\\\"from\\\":{\\\"x\\\":x1,\\\"y\\\":y1}, \\\"to\\\":{\\\"x\\\":x2,\\\"y\\\":y2}}`. `text` names a node the\\n   way the platform does — its text, else its content description — so a React\\n   Native pressable (`accessibilityLabel`, e.g. a bottom tab) is reachable by the\\n   label you read on screen. `content_desc` and `id` are the *explicit* fields:\\n   use them when the label lives in a content description while the node's text\\n   is something else (an RN `TextInput` whose text is its placeholder), or simply\\n   when you want the model to be exact. A selector that matches **several** nodes\\n   the user can see is refused (`AMBIGUOUS_SELECTOR`, `error.details.candidates`\\n   lists every match with id/text/content_desc/bounds) — narrow it with\\n   `content_desc`/`id`/`bounds`, or pass `index`; nothing is tapped on a guess.\\n   A node that is not visible to the user is never tapped: `ELEMENT_OFFSCREEN`\\n   names its bounds. **The reply already says what changed** (FB3): `changes`\\n   carries the settled tree diff of the action — `added`, `removed` and\\n   `changed` nodes (each with `id`, `text`, `content_desc`, `class`, `bounds`; a\\n   changed node also lists the `fields` that changed, old → new), plus the\\n   current `activity` and the `focused` node. A normal step is one call, not\\n   tap-then-observe; call `acos_observe_tree` only when you need the whole tree\\n   or bounds you have not seen (`diff: false` restores the old reply).\\n   `state_changed: false` with `accepted: true` means the event was delivered\\n   but the screen did not change — read the in-band `note` and `changes`.\\n\\n   **Scrolling a list (REL3S).** The ART engine publishes a real gesture, not a\\n   jump: `DOWN`, one `MOVE` every ~16 ms (the display refresh cadence) with real\\n   event timestamps, then `UP`, on the same input channel a tap uses. The app's\\n   own `VelocityTracker`/`OverScroller` therefore decide the fling, so a\\n   `ListView`/`RecyclerView`/RN `FlatList` scrolls exactly as it does on a phone.\\n   Three ways to name the gesture:\\n\\n   ```\\n   acos_swipe {\\\"from\\\": {\\\"x\\\": 300, \\\"y\\\": 1100}, \\\"to\\\": {\\\"x\\\": 300, \\\"y\\\": 400},\\n               \\\"duration_ms\\\": 300, \\\"fling\\\": true}\\n   acos_swipe {\\\"direction\\\": \\\"up\\\", \\\"distance\\\": 900, \\\"duration_ms\\\": 300}\\n   acos_swipe {\\\"direction\\\": \\\"up\\\", \\\"fling\\\": true, \\\"until\\\": {\\\"text\\\": \\\"Row 80\\\"}}\\n   ```\\n\\n   `direction` (`up`/`down`/`left`/`right`) gestures across the display centre;\\n   `up` moves the finger up, which scrolls the content up and brings later rows\\n   into view. `duration_ms` defaults to 400 (120 with `fling: true`); a slow\\n   drag follows the finger, a fling coasts on the list's own physics.\\n\\n   **`until` is `scroll_until`.** With a selector, the call repeats the swipe\\n   (bounded by `max_attempts`, default 6, 10 with `fling`) and after **every**\\n   swipe takes a fresh **settled** traversal and looks for the node. `until.found`\\n   is only ever reported from such a traversal (`until.attempts`,\\n   `until.frame_seq` name it). **Two consumed swipes that move nothing end the\\n   search** (REL3H2): when the settled tree before and after two *consumed*\\n   gestures in a row is identical, the list has reached its end and the call\\n   fails with `END_OF_LIST` (`error.details.end_of_list: true`, `attempts`,\\n   `max_attempts`, `unchanged_gestures`, `last_tree_texts`) instead of spending\\n   the remaining attempts. One unchanged gesture is deliberately not enough: a\\n   list that is still loading is unchanged for a gesture too. **A swipe the app\\n   did not consume** (`finishInputEvent(handled) = false`, or a settle with\\n   `reason: unhandled_input`) moved nothing because it missed the scroller — that\\n   is `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND` with the hint \\\"gesture not consumed (swipe missed the\\n   scroller?)\\\", never `END_OF_LIST`. Otherwise a node that never appears fails\\n   with `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND`, `error.details.attempts`,\\n   `error.details.max_attempts` and `error.details.last_tree_texts` (what the\\n   list actually showed at the end) — never a pass, and never a guess from a\\n   cached tree. `until` requires the\\n   strict settle; `settle: fast`/`content` is refused because neither can prove a\\n   settled observation.\\n\\n   `handled` works per event: a scrolling gesture reports\\n   `handled_evidence.published` equal to `acked` (a 300 ms swipe is ~20 events,\\n   not 2) and `unhandled` lists any event the window dropped — e.g. a gesture\\n   that lands on a non-scrollable view.\\n\\n   **The same two steps in a scenario (REL3S).** A scenario scrolls with the\\n   same gesture and the same search:\\n\\n   ```yaml\\n   - swipe: {direction: up, distance: 900, duration_ms: 300, fling: true}\\n   - scroll_until: {selector: {text: {exact: \\\"Row 80\\\"}}, fling: true, max_attempts: 12}\\n   - tap: {selector: {text: {exact: \\\"Row 80\\\"}}}\\n   ```\\n\\n   `swipe` takes `from`/`to` points or `direction` (+ `distance`), `duration_ms`,\\n   `fling`, the per-step `settle`/`settle_timeout_ms` rules and SE1's\\n   `expect_change`. `scroll_until` takes the same geometry plus the `selector`\\n   to find and `max_attempts` (1..50); it always runs strict. Both report REL3E's\\n   `input` block (`handled`, `handled_evidence`, `target`), and `scroll_until`\\n   also reports `input.until {selector, found, end_of_list, attempts, frame_seq}`;\\n   a miss fails the step with `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND` (every attempt used, or the\\n   swipe was not consumed) or `END_OF_LIST` (two consumed swipes moved nothing:\\n   the list ended) plus `details.attempts` / `details.last_tree_texts`. The next\\n   `assert_*` binds to the swipe/search's own settled frame.\\n   Identity rule, compared fields and the 40-entry cap: `docs/MCP.md` §1c.\\n   **`handled` is the app's own answer, and it is the one you branch on.**\\n   Every `acos_tap`/`acos_key`/`acos_text`/`acos_swipe` reply (and every\\n   scenario `tap`/`type`/`key`/`back` step JSON, under `input`) carries\\n   `handled`: the bit the app window passed to Android's\\n   `InputEventReceiver.finishInputEvent(handled)`. It is the framework's\\n   verdict, not a guess from pixels:\\n\\n   - `handled: false` — **nothing in the app consumed the input.** A tap on a\\n     label whose row is not pressable (the switch beside it is) is exactly this:\\n     `state_changed: false` is then the correct, expected outcome, not a broken\\n     tap. The same reply's `target` names what happened: `target.node` is the\\n     node the selector resolved to, `target.hit.node` is what the injected point\\n     actually hit (its `class`, `text`/`content_desc`, `bounds`, `clickable`),\\n     `target.hit.consumer` is the nearest clickable ancestor-or-self (`null`\\n     when there is none), and `target.hit.suggest` is the nearest clickable node\\n     off that branch — the switch, the row's pressable, the button. A\\n     `handled_hint` spells it out: *\\\"nothing tappable there … tap the nearest\\n     clickable android.widget.Switch … instead\\\"*.\\n   - `handled: true` + `state_changed: false` — the app **did** consume it and\\n     the settled frame still shows no change: the handler may be asynchronous,\\n     or the control already had that state. `handled_hint` says so. Assert on\\n     the state you expect rather than expecting a change.\\n   - `handled: true` + `state_changed: true` — the press was consumed and the\\n     screen changed; `changes` says what.\\n   - `handled: null` — the guest did not ack every event\\n     (`handled_evidence.acked` < `published`): **unknown**, never reported as\\n     \\\"not handled\\\". `handled_evidence` always travels with the boolean\\n     (`source`, `published`, `acked`, `unhandled` seqs).\\n   - `handled: true` + `handled_evidence.source: \\\"host_window_removed\\\"` — the\\n     window the input was delivered to was removed during the step (a dialog\\n     dismissed by `back`). Its `InputEventReceiver` is disposed before it can\\n     finish the event, so the host's removal is the proof and it outranks an\\n     all-unhandled ack set (`source` is then `host_window_removed`, never\\n     `guest`, and the step carries no `INPUT_UNHANDLED` warning). `source` is\\n     `guest` when an ack answered, `none` when nothing was delivered.\\n\\n   Two consequences worth knowing. (a) `state_changed` is evidence about the\\n   screen; `handled` is evidence about the app. An agent that only reads\\n   `state_changed` cannot tell \\\"nothing tappable there\\\" from \\\"handled, nothing\\n   changed\\\" — read `handled` first. (b) The hit-test in `target.hit` is\\n   computed from the tree the tap resolved against (visible rects, deepest\\n   node first); it tells you *what is there*. `handled` tells you *whether the\\n   app took it*. When they disagree (`hit.clickable: true`, `handled: false`)\\n   the control was hit but refused the press — disabled, or a parent\\n   intercepted it.\\n6. **Read the app's own words.** `acos_logs` returns the guest log buffer and\\n   also `launched` / `stage_reached`, so an empty buffer is only clean if the\\n   app actually reached `onCreate`. The **default view is `min_priority: \\\"W\\\"`\\n   plus the app's own package** (records at warning or above, plus anything that\\n   names the app), which keeps SoLoader/framework noise out of the reply. The\\n   full buffer stays in `logs/logcat.txt` (a live reply names `log_path`),\\n   `\\\"all\\\": true` or `min_priority: \\\"V\\\"` returns all of it, and `tag` narrows\\n   further.\\n7. **Prove the flow.** `acos_verify {\\\"apk\\\": ..., \\\"scenario\\\": ...}` (or\\n   `acos verify <scenario.yaml> --artifacts out/<name>`) runs a YAML scenario and\\n   writes `verdict.json`, per-step JSON and the frames. The tool runs **real\\n   ART by default** (`engine: \\\"art\\\"`); pass `\\\"engine\\\": \\\"rust-vm\\\"` only to\\n   reproduce the frozen Rust VM, and the reply's `fidelity` then says\\n   `native-runtime`. With `required_fidelity: \\\"art-reference\\\"` a non-ART run is\\n   refused as `UNSUPPORTED_BACKEND` (achieved fidelity does not satisfy), never\\n   passed. A pass is valid only when its evidence exists on disk.\\n8. **Clean up.** `acos_reset {\\\"scope\\\": \\\"app\\\"}` (relaunch in a fresh guest with\\n   an empty app-data directory) and `acos_destroy` (idempotent). Sessions also\\n   die with the `acos-mcp` process.\\n\\n### Recipe: type into a search field\\n\\nA live session can type — the same path a scenario `type` step uses — so a\\nsearch flow no longer has to become a scenario. In four calls:\\n\\n```\\n1. acos_observe_tree                      # read the field's name from the tree\\n2. acos_tap {\\\"content_desc\\\": \\\"Search Japanese dictionary\\\"}\\n3. acos_text {\\\"text\\\": \\\"kayou\\\"}\\n4. acos_observe_tree                      # the query is in the field, results below\\n```\\n\\n* **Pick the field by `content_desc`, not by `text`.** A React Native\\n  `TextInput` reports its *placeholder* as `text` (AOSP's accessibility mapping\\n  takes the hint when the field is empty) and keeps the accessibility label in\\n  `content_desc`. So `acos_tap {\\\"text\\\": \\\"Search Japanese dictionary\\\"}` is\\n  `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND` while `acos_tap {\\\"content_desc\\\": \\\"...\\\"}` hits the field —\\n  the same value works in a scenario selector.\\n* **Tap first, then type.** Typing goes to the focused editor. The tap's\\n  `accepted: true` + `state_changed: true` is the delivery evidence; the next\\n  tree's `focused: true` on the field node is the focus evidence.\\n* **No class check is needed.** RN inputs are obfuscated subclasses\\n  (`Z4.g`), not `android.widget.EditText`; `acos_text` does not care what the\\n  class is called — it sends the guest's `type_text` command and judges the\\n  settled result, exactly like `type:` in YAML.\\n* **The tree proves it.** After `acos_text {\\\"text\\\": \\\"kayou\\\"}` on Gaten, the next\\n  tree carries `kayou` in the field and the result list (`19 results`, `歌謡`,\\n  `かよう`, ...). Assert on those texts (or in a scenario with\\n  `assert_text`) — never on the call's `status` alone.\\n* **The reply names the field, and masks unless it is known to be plain.**\\n  `acos_text`'s `result.target.node` is the focused field the input went to, as\\n  the tree shows it. The typed text is echoed (`target.text`) only when that\\n  field is focused and is not a password. Otherwise the reply carries\\n  `target.text_masked: true`, `target.chars` (the honest count), `target.text:\\n  \\\"*\\\"` and a `reason`: a focused password field, `mask: true`, or an\\n  **unknown target** (the tree names no focused field; nothing is guessed, and no\\n  other field is ever reported in its place). Masking only hides the echo: the\\n  tree still shows what the app displays, so prove typing with the next\\n  `acos_observe_tree`, not with the reply.\",\"guide_all_topic\":\"all\",\"guide_source\":\"docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md\",\"guide_topic\":\"workflow\",\"guide_topics\":[\"overview\",\"workflow\",\"cli\",\"scenarios\",\"errors\",\"limits\",\"mcp\"],\"guide_unknown_topic\":false,\"loop\":\"inspect -> verify -> read verdict.json -> fix -> rerun\",\"markdown\":\"# ACOS via MCP\\n\\nLoop: **inspect -> verify -> read verdict -> fix -> rerun**.\\n\\n## Currently available tools\\n\\n- `acos_inspect`\\n- `acos_run_dex`\\n- `acos_run`\\n- `acos_observe`\\n- `acos_action`\\n- `acos_reset`\\n- `acos_destroy`\\n- `acos_verify`\\n- `acos_capabilities`\\n- `acos_help`\\n- `acos_launch`\\n- `acos_observe_tree`\\n- `acos_tap`\\n- `acos_key`\\n- `acos_text`\\n- `acos_swipe`\\n- `acos_logs`\\n- `acos_status`\\n- `acos_doctor`\\n\\n## Not implemented yet (returns `unsupported`, never a pass)\\n\\n- `acos_capture` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\\n- `acos_assert` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\\n- `acos_events` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\\n- `acos_profile` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\\n- `acos_capability_report` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\\n\\nCall `acos_help` with a topic for the full playbook, or `acos_capabilities` to re-probe.\\n\",\"supported\":[\"acos_inspect\",\"acos_run_dex\",\"acos_run\",\"acos_observe\",\"acos_action\",\"acos_reset\",\"acos_destroy\",\"acos_verify\",\"acos_capabilities\",\"acos_help\",\"acos_launch\",\"acos_observe_tree\",\"acos_tap\",\"acos_key\",\"acos_text\",\"acos_swipe\",\"acos_logs\",\"acos_status\",\"acos_doctor\"],\"unsupported\":[{\"command\":\"capture\",\"fidelity_when_available\":\"native-runtime\",\"reason\":\"the acos backend does not implement this command yet\",\"tool\":\"acos_capture\"},{\"command\":\"assert\",\"fidelity_when_available\":\"native-runtime\",\"reason\":\"the acos backend does not implement this command yet\",\"tool\":\"acos_assert\"},{\"command\":\"events\",\"fidelity_when_available\":\"native-runtime\",\"reason\":\"the acos backend does not implement this command yet\",\"tool\":\"acos_events\"},{\"command\":\"profile\",\"fidelity_when_available\":\"native-runtime\",\"reason\":\"the acos backend does not implement this command yet\",\"tool\":\"acos_profile\"},{\"command\":\"capability-report\",\"fidelity_when_available\":\"static-analysis\",\"reason\":\"the acos backend does not implement this command yet\",\"tool\":\"acos_capability_report\"}],\"workflow\":[\"1. acos_inspect the APK (static only; never executes) to learn package, launcher, SDK, DEX/native inventory.\",\"2. acos_capability_report (once implemented) or acos_capabilities to check the engine can do what the task needs.\",\"3. acos_verify an APK + scenario into an artifacts directory; the verdict and step evidence are the product.\",\"4. Read verdict.json and steps/NN-*.json on a non-pass; never guess.\",\"5. Fix the app or the scenario, then rerun into a fresh directory. Reuse the same scenario so the comparison is meaningful.\",\"6. A pass is valid only when its evidence exists; `unsupported` is never a pass.\"]},\"status\":\"pass\"}","type":"text"}],"isError":false,"structuredContent":{"backend":"acos-native","capabilities_used":[],"fidelity":"static-analysis","metrics":{"wall_ms":0},"ok":true,"request_id":"req_help","result":{"available_command_fidelity":[{"fidelity":"static-analysis","tool":"acos_inspect"},{"fidelity":"native-runtime","tool":"acos_run_dex"},{"fidelity":"native-runtime","tool":"acos_run"},{"fidelity":"native-runtime","tool":"acos_observe"},{"fidelity":"native-runtime","tool":"acos_action"},{"fidelity":"native-runtime","tool":"acos_reset"},{"fidelity":"native-runtime","tool":"acos_destroy"},{"fidelity":"native-runtime","tool":"acos_verify"}],"backend":{"binary":"/home/Qui/.t3/worktrees/acos/t3-9fde79f7-5b90a224f291fea1/.cache/rg1-prefix/share/acos/0.3.0/bin/acos","probe_error":null,"probed":true,"version":"acos 0.3.0"},"compatibility":null,"default_command_fidelity":"static-analysis","error_playbook":[{"action":"Do not rewrite the app to hide the gap. Read details, try another fidelity/target, or report unsupported. Never a pass.","category":"engine cannot do it (status=unsupported)","codes":["UNSUPPORTED_ANDROID_API","UNSUPPORTED_JAVA_API","UNSUPPORTED_BYTECODE","UNSUPPORTED_NATIVE_SYMBOL","UNSUPPORTED_RELOCATION","NEEDS_REAL_DEVICE","REQUIRES_GMS","WRONG_ABI","NO_DISPLAY","NO_TEXT","UNSUPPORTED_BACKEND","RENDER_UNSUPPORTED_OP"]},{"action":"Read the assertion diff (expected/actual/path) and the linked frame/tree, then fix the app or the expectation and rerun.","category":"the app is wrong (status=fail)","codes":["ASSERTION_FAILED","ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND"]},{"action":"Read the crash/ANR event and last actions; fix the app or the unsupported platform call. Never a pass.","category":"app crashed or hung","codes":["APP_CRASHED","APP_NOT_RESPONDING","JAVA_EXCEPTION"]},{"action":"Rebuild/re-sign/re-package and retry inspect first; these are artifact problems, not app verdicts.","category":"bad build artifact","codes":["APK_NOT_ZIP","APK_NO_MANIFEST","APK_IO","APK_ENTRY","APK_SIGNING_MALFORMED","AXML_MALFORMED","ARSC_MALFORMED","DEX_BAD_*","DEX_TRUNCATED","DEX_VERIFY_FAILED","DEX_LOAD_ERROR","ELF_MALFORMED","NATIVE_DEP_NOT_FOUND","NATIVE_INIT_FAILED","NATIVE_SYMBOL_NOT_FOUND"]},{"action":"Create the session first, serialise mutations, re-observe after mutations, reduce concurrency or raise an explicit budget.","category":"session / resource problems","codes":["SESSION_NOT_FOUND","SESSION_BUSY","STALE_REF","TIMEOUT","RESOURCE_LIMIT","RAM_GUARD","RAM_ABORT"]},{"action":"Wait for a new frame, use after_seq/after_cause, re-run at a qualifying fidelity, and write artifacts inside the artifacts directory.","category":"evidence / fidelity problems","codes":["NO_FRESH_FRAME","UNVERIFIED_FIDELITY","ARTIFACTS_INVALID","UNSAFE_PATH"]},{"action":"Fix every reported path/field; choose an isolation level the engine supports or report the missing guarantee.","category":"invalid input","codes":["INVALID_SCENARIO","INVALID_ARGUMENT","ISOLATION_UNAVAILABLE","ISOLATION_UNSATISFIED"]},{"action":"An ACOS bug or strict-rendering rejection. Capture request/scenario/events; do not retry blindly or claim pass.","category":"harness failure","codes":["VM_INTERNAL","INTERNAL","RENDER_*","RENDER_STRICT"]}],"fidelity_rules":[{"level":"static-analysis","proves":"host APK/DEX parsing only; no runtime behaviour"},{"level":"jvm-reference","proves":"host OpenJDK pure-Java semantics only; never Android/ART/framework/JNI/rendering"},{"level":"native-runtime","proves":"ACOS executed it; not ART-validated unless validated_against says so"},{"level":"art-reference","proves":"real ART on a reference image; authoritative for the stated API/ABI"},{"level":"physical-device","proves":"a real device/OEM target"},{"level":"simulated","proves":"the mock path only; not an Android success"},{"level":"n/a","proves":"nothing about app behaviour: this is a meta reply (help/capabilities/doctor) about ACOS itself"}],"guide":"## Recipes (answer these first)\n\n**Take a screenshot.** There is no capture tool on the MCP surface yet. Two\nroutes work today: (a) a `capture: {name: shot, format: png, fresh: true}` step\nin an `acos_verify` scenario — the PNG lands as `frames/<seq>.png` under the\nartifacts directory and the step JSON names it; (b) with the CLI on a live\ndaemon session, `acos observe --screenshot out/shot.png --json`. Coming:\n`screenshot: true` on `acos_observe_tree`, which returns the PNG path in the\nreply (and `NO_FRESH_FRAME` instead of a silent no-op when the frame carries no\npixels).\n\n**Type into a field.** Observe the tree, tap the field (`acos_tap {\"id\": ...}`\nor `{\"text\": ...}`), type with `acos_text {\"text\": \"Ada\"}`, then observe again to\nread the value. Text goes to the *focused* editor through the real\n`InputConnection`; `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND` with `details.kind = NoFocusedEditor`\nmeans the tap did not land on an editable field. If `acos_text` answers\n`UNSUPPORTED_BACKEND` on a live session, the same interaction as a scenario\n`type:` step runs through `acos_verify` and does work.\n\n**Find a control by `content_desc`.** `acos_observe_tree` returns `text` **and**\n`content_desc` per node; icon buttons usually carry their name only in\n`content_desc` (for example `Search Japanese dictionary`). Select it in a\nscenario with `selector: {content_desc: {contains: Search}}`, or hand the label\nto `acos_tap {\"text\": ...}` (text or content-desc), or tap the centre of the\nnode's `bounds` with `x`/`y`. Never read a coordinate off a PNG: PNG space is\noffset by `frame.display_origin`.\n\n**Prove a flow.** Put the interaction in a YAML scenario (`topic: \"scenarios\"`)\nand run `acos_verify` (real ART by default). The pass counts only with its\nevidence on disk: `verdict.json`, `steps/NN-*.json` and the referenced frames —\nand `expect_change: true` on the taps whose point is to change the screen is\nwhat makes the pass mean something.\n\n**Launch arguments.** Paths must be **absolute**: the server's cwd is not your\nshell's, and a relative path is `INVALID_ARGUMENT` with\n`details.resolved_path`. Each launch rung carries `ms`. Pass `\"label\": \"...\"`\nand, if you will park the session, `\"idle_ttl_s\": N`: the server destroys its\nown sessions after the idle TTL (default 600 s, `ACOS_SESSION_IDLE_TTL_S`) and\nthe next call on an expired one returns `SESSION_NOT_FOUND` with\n`details.reason=idle_ttl_expired` and the TTL.\n\n**Assert, do not eyeball.** `assert_text` (optionally with `bounds`),\n`assert_visible` / `assert_not_visible` (collapsed content stays in the tree with\n`visible: false`, so assert visibility, never presence) and `assert_log` turn a\nvisual claim into a machine check with a diff.\nA step settles on the screen its action produced; a change the app makes **later** (a delayed post, a worker thread, a timer) may land after the step settled. Check such an effect with `assert_text` + `timeout_ms` (a real wait), not with the action step's result.\n\n## The loop\n\n```\ndoctor -> inspect -> launch -> observe_tree -> tap/text/key/swipe -> logs -> verify -> reset -> destroy\n```\n\n1. **Preflight the host.** `acos doctor --json` (CLI) or `acos_doctor` (MCP).\n   It checks the Android SDK, `d8`, `android.jar`, the JDK, the workspace\n   helper, the signing keystore, the boot-classpath cache and host memory, and\n   every failing check carries a `fix:` line. Fix those first: without them a\n   later failure is a host problem wearing an app's clothes.\n2. **Know the APK before running it.** `acos_inspect` / `acos inspect <apk>`\n   is static only (package, launcher activity, min/target SDK, DEX and native\n   inventory). It never executes the app and is never proof of runtime\n   behaviour. It also cross-checks the **native libraries the app asks for**\n   against the ones the APK ships: `result.native_lib_check.warnings` lists\n   `{\"code\": \"NATIVE_LIB_MISSING\", \"lib\": \"libworklets.so\", \"abi\": \"x86_64\",\n   \"source\": \"elf-needed\"|\"dex-load-library\", \"referenced_by\": [...]}` for every\n   library named by a bundled library's `DT_NEEDED` or by a\n   `System.loadLibrary`/`SoLoader.loadLibrary` call site in the DEX that has no\n   `lib/<abi>/` entry. Platform libraries (`libc.so`, `libm.so`, …) are never\n   warnings, and a healthy APK answers `warnings: []` — so a non-empty list is a\n   **hint**, not the cause: it says \"this library is referenced and absent\", not\n   \"this is why the app misbehaves\". Treat it as the first thing to check before\n   debugging the UI, then confirm at runtime.\n3. **Run it.** `acos_launch {\"apk\": \"...\"}` (MCP, session persists) or\n   `acos launch <apk> --engine art --json`. **The MCP session tools always run\n   real ART** (the server starts its own private `acos serve --engine art`\n   daemon); the `acos` CLI's own default route is the frozen Rust VM, so pass\n   `--engine art` there. Read the ladder:\n   `apk_parsed -> process_booted -> bind_application -> application_on_create ->\n   client_transaction -> activity_on_create -> on_resume -> first_frame`.\n   `reached_stage` is the honest answer to \"how far did it get\"; the first\n   rung that is not `ok` carries the blocker. **A launch that gets a window but\n   never mounts the app's own content is `blocked` with\n   `error.code = PLACEHOLDER_CONTENT`**, never `pass`/`first_frame`: the reply's\n   `result.content` says `{\"mounted\": false, \"reason\": \"placeholder-content\"}`,\n   `stage_reached` is `window`, and `error.details.log_hint` quotes the one guest\n   log line that explains it (an `UnsatisfiedLinkError`, a `SoLoader` line, a\n   `couldn't find` line, else the first `E` line; if the log says nothing the\n   hint says so instead of guessing). The usual cause is a native library the\n   APK references and does not ship — `acos_inspect`'s\n   `native_lib_check.warnings` names it statically. Do not retry the same APK\n   with a longer timeout; it is the packaging.\n4. **Observe before acting.** `acos_observe_tree` returns nodes with\n   `resource_id`, `text`, `content_desc`, `class`, `bounds`,\n   `clickable/enabled/focused/scrollable`. Use those bounds and ids for the\n   next step. Before the app has a window the reply is `unsupported` with\n   `details.reason = \"not_ready\"` — never an empty tree.\n5. **Act.** `acos_tap {\"text\": \"Increment\"}` (preferred),\n   `acos_tap {\"content_desc\": \"Search Japanese dictionary\"}`,\n   `acos_tap {\"id\": \"inc\"}`, `acos_tap {\"x\":.., \"y\":..}`,\n   `acos_key {\"code\": \"back\"}`, `acos_text {\"text\": \"hello\"}` (types into the\n   focused editor — the same guest `type_text` command a scenario `type` step\n   sends), `acos_swipe {\"from\":{\"x\":x1,\"y\":y1}, \"to\":{\"x\":x2,\"y\":y2}}`. `text` names a node the\n   way the platform does — its text, else its content description — so a React\n   Native pressable (`accessibilityLabel`, e.g. a bottom tab) is reachable by the\n   label you read on screen. `content_desc` and `id` are the *explicit* fields:\n   use them when the label lives in a content description while the node's text\n   is something else (an RN `TextInput` whose text is its placeholder), or simply\n   when you want the model to be exact. A selector that matches **several** nodes\n   the user can see is refused (`AMBIGUOUS_SELECTOR`, `error.details.candidates`\n   lists every match with id/text/content_desc/bounds) — narrow it with\n   `content_desc`/`id`/`bounds`, or pass `index`; nothing is tapped on a guess.\n   A node that is not visible to the user is never tapped: `ELEMENT_OFFSCREEN`\n   names its bounds. **The reply already says what changed** (FB3): `changes`\n   carries the settled tree diff of the action — `added`, `removed` and\n   `changed` nodes (each with `id`, `text`, `content_desc`, `class`, `bounds`; a\n   changed node also lists the `fields` that changed, old → new), plus the\n   current `activity` and the `focused` node. A normal step is one call, not\n   tap-then-observe; call `acos_observe_tree` only when you need the whole tree\n   or bounds you have not seen (`diff: false` restores the old reply).\n   `state_changed: false` with `accepted: true` means the event was delivered\n   but the screen did not change — read the in-band `note` and `changes`.\n\n   **Scrolling a list (REL3S).** The ART engine publishes a real gesture, not a\n   jump: `DOWN`, one `MOVE` every ~16 ms (the display refresh cadence) with real\n   event timestamps, then `UP`, on the same input channel a tap uses. The app's\n   own `VelocityTracker`/`OverScroller` therefore decide the fling, so a\n   `ListView`/`RecyclerView`/RN `FlatList` scrolls exactly as it does on a phone.\n   Three ways to name the gesture:\n\n   ```\n   acos_swipe {\"from\": {\"x\": 300, \"y\": 1100}, \"to\": {\"x\": 300, \"y\": 400},\n               \"duration_ms\": 300, \"fling\": true}\n   acos_swipe {\"direction\": \"up\", \"distance\": 900, \"duration_ms\": 300}\n   acos_swipe {\"direction\": \"up\", \"fling\": true, \"until\": {\"text\": \"Row 80\"}}\n   ```\n\n   `direction` (`up`/`down`/`left`/`right`) gestures across the display centre;\n   `up` moves the finger up, which scrolls the content up and brings later rows\n   into view. `duration_ms` defaults to 400 (120 with `fling: true`); a slow\n   drag follows the finger, a fling coasts on the list's own physics.\n\n   **`until` is `scroll_until`.** With a selector, the call repeats the swipe\n   (bounded by `max_attempts`, default 6, 10 with `fling`) and after **every**\n   swipe takes a fresh **settled** traversal and looks for the node. `until.found`\n   is only ever reported from such a traversal (`until.attempts`,\n   `until.frame_seq` name it). **Two consumed swipes that move nothing end the\n   search** (REL3H2): when the settled tree before and after two *consumed*\n   gestures in a row is identical, the list has reached its end and the call\n   fails with `END_OF_LIST` (`error.details.end_of_list: true`, `attempts`,\n   `max_attempts`, `unchanged_gestures`, `last_tree_texts`) instead of spending\n   the remaining attempts. One unchanged gesture is deliberately not enough: a\n   list that is still loading is unchanged for a gesture too. **A swipe the app\n   did not consume** (`finishInputEvent(handled) = false`, or a settle with\n   `reason: unhandled_input`) moved nothing because it missed the scroller — that\n   is `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND` with the hint \"gesture not consumed (swipe missed the\n   scroller?)\", never `END_OF_LIST`. Otherwise a node that never appears fails\n   with `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND`, `error.details.attempts`,\n   `error.details.max_attempts` and `error.details.last_tree_texts` (what the\n   list actually showed at the end) — never a pass, and never a guess from a\n   cached tree. `until` requires the\n   strict settle; `settle: fast`/`content` is refused because neither can prove a\n   settled observation.\n\n   `handled` works per event: a scrolling gesture reports\n   `handled_evidence.published` equal to `acked` (a 300 ms swipe is ~20 events,\n   not 2) and `unhandled` lists any event the window dropped — e.g. a gesture\n   that lands on a non-scrollable view.\n\n   **The same two steps in a scenario (REL3S).** A scenario scrolls with the\n   same gesture and the same search:\n\n   ```yaml\n   - swipe: {direction: up, distance: 900, duration_ms: 300, fling: true}\n   - scroll_until: {selector: {text: {exact: \"Row 80\"}}, fling: true, max_attempts: 12}\n   - tap: {selector: {text: {exact: \"Row 80\"}}}\n   ```\n\n   `swipe` takes `from`/`to` points or `direction` (+ `distance`), `duration_ms`,\n   `fling`, the per-step `settle`/`settle_timeout_ms` rules and SE1's\n   `expect_change`. `scroll_until` takes the same geometry plus the `selector`\n   to find and `max_attempts` (1..50); it always runs strict. Both report REL3E's\n   `input` block (`handled`, `handled_evidence`, `target`), and `scroll_until`\n   also reports `input.until {selector, found, end_of_list, attempts, frame_seq}`;\n   a miss fails the step with `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND` (every attempt used, or the\n   swipe was not consumed) or `END_OF_LIST` (two consumed swipes moved nothing:\n   the list ended) plus `details.attempts` / `details.last_tree_texts`. The next\n   `assert_*` binds to the swipe/search's own settled frame.\n   Identity rule, compared fields and the 40-entry cap: `docs/MCP.md` §1c.\n   **`handled` is the app's own answer, and it is the one you branch on.**\n   Every `acos_tap`/`acos_key`/`acos_text`/`acos_swipe` reply (and every\n   scenario `tap`/`type`/`key`/`back` step JSON, under `input`) carries\n   `handled`: the bit the app window passed to Android's\n   `InputEventReceiver.finishInputEvent(handled)`. It is the framework's\n   verdict, not a guess from pixels:\n\n   - `handled: false` — **nothing in the app consumed the input.** A tap on a\n     label whose row is not pressable (the switch beside it is) is exactly this:\n     `state_changed: false` is then the correct, expected outcome, not a broken\n     tap. The same reply's `target` names what happened: `target.node` is the\n     node the selector resolved to, `target.hit.node` is what the injected point\n     actually hit (its `class`, `text`/`content_desc`, `bounds`, `clickable`),\n     `target.hit.consumer` is the nearest clickable ancestor-or-self (`null`\n     when there is none), and `target.hit.suggest` is the nearest clickable node\n     off that branch — the switch, the row's pressable, the button. A\n     `handled_hint` spells it out: *\"nothing tappable there … tap the nearest\n     clickable android.widget.Switch … instead\"*.\n   - `handled: true` + `state_changed: false` — the app **did** consume it and\n     the settled frame still shows no change: the handler may be asynchronous,\n     or the control already had that state. `handled_hint` says so. Assert on\n     the state you expect rather than expecting a change.\n   - `handled: true` + `state_changed: true` — the press was consumed and the\n     screen changed; `changes` says what.\n   - `handled: null` — the guest did not ack every event\n     (`handled_evidence.acked` < `published`): **unknown**, never reported as\n     \"not handled\". `handled_evidence` always travels with the boolean\n     (`source`, `published`, `acked`, `unhandled` seqs).\n   - `handled: true` + `handled_evidence.source: \"host_window_removed\"` — the\n     window the input was delivered to was removed during the step (a dialog\n     dismissed by `back`). Its `InputEventReceiver` is disposed before it can\n     finish the event, so the host's removal is the proof and it outranks an\n     all-unhandled ack set (`source` is then `host_window_removed`, never\n     `guest`, and the step carries no `INPUT_UNHANDLED` warning). `source` is\n     `guest` when an ack answered, `none` when nothing was delivered.\n\n   Two consequences worth knowing. (a) `state_changed` is evidence about the\n   screen; `handled` is evidence about the app. An agent that only reads\n   `state_changed` cannot tell \"nothing tappable there\" from \"handled, nothing\n   changed\" — read `handled` first. (b) The hit-test in `target.hit` is\n   computed from the tree the tap resolved against (visible rects, deepest\n   node first); it tells you *what is there*. `handled` tells you *whether the\n   app took it*. When they disagree (`hit.clickable: true`, `handled: false`)\n   the control was hit but refused the press — disabled, or a parent\n   intercepted it.\n6. **Read the app's own words.** `acos_logs` returns the guest log buffer and\n   also `launched` / `stage_reached`, so an empty buffer is only clean if the\n   app actually reached `onCreate`. The **default view is `min_priority: \"W\"`\n   plus the app's own package** (records at warning or above, plus anything that\n   names the app), which keeps SoLoader/framework noise out of the reply. The\n   full buffer stays in `logs/logcat.txt` (a live reply names `log_path`),\n   `\"all\": true` or `min_priority: \"V\"` returns all of it, and `tag` narrows\n   further.\n7. **Prove the flow.** `acos_verify {\"apk\": ..., \"scenario\": ...}` (or\n   `acos verify <scenario.yaml> --artifacts out/<name>`) runs a YAML scenario and\n   writes `verdict.json`, per-step JSON and the frames. The tool runs **real\n   ART by default** (`engine: \"art\"`); pass `\"engine\": \"rust-vm\"` only to\n   reproduce the frozen Rust VM, and the reply's `fidelity` then says\n   `native-runtime`. With `required_fidelity: \"art-reference\"` a non-ART run is\n   refused as `UNSUPPORTED_BACKEND` (achieved fidelity does not satisfy), never\n   passed. A pass is valid only when its evidence exists on disk.\n8. **Clean up.** `acos_reset {\"scope\": \"app\"}` (relaunch in a fresh guest with\n   an empty app-data directory) and `acos_destroy` (idempotent). Sessions also\n   die with the `acos-mcp` process.\n\n### Recipe: type into a search field\n\nA live session can type — the same path a scenario `type` step uses — so a\nsearch flow no longer has to become a scenario. In four calls:\n\n```\n1. acos_observe_tree                      # read the field's name from the tree\n2. acos_tap {\"content_desc\": \"Search Japanese dictionary\"}\n3. acos_text {\"text\": \"kayou\"}\n4. acos_observe_tree                      # the query is in the field, results below\n```\n\n* **Pick the field by `content_desc`, not by `text`.** A React Native\n  `TextInput` reports its *placeholder* as `text` (AOSP's accessibility mapping\n  takes the hint when the field is empty) and keeps the accessibility label in\n  `content_desc`. So `acos_tap {\"text\": \"Search Japanese dictionary\"}` is\n  `ELEMENT_NOT_FOUND` while `acos_tap {\"content_desc\": \"...\"}` hits the field —\n  the same value works in a scenario selector.\n* **Tap first, then type.** Typing goes to the focused editor. The tap's\n  `accepted: true` + `state_changed: true` is the delivery evidence; the next\n  tree's `focused: true` on the field node is the focus evidence.\n* **No class check is needed.** RN inputs are obfuscated subclasses\n  (`Z4.g`), not `android.widget.EditText`; `acos_text` does not care what the\n  class is called — it sends the guest's `type_text` command and judges the\n  settled result, exactly like `type:` in YAML.\n* **The tree proves it.** After `acos_text {\"text\": \"kayou\"}` on Gaten, the next\n  tree carries `kayou` in the field and the result list (`19 results`, `歌謡`,\n  `かよう`, ...). Assert on those texts (or in a scenario with\n  `assert_text`) — never on the call's `status` alone.\n* **The reply names the field, and masks unless it is known to be plain.**\n  `acos_text`'s `result.target.node` is the focused field the input went to, as\n  the tree shows it. The typed text is echoed (`target.text`) only when that\n  field is focused and is not a password. Otherwise the reply carries\n  `target.text_masked: true`, `target.chars` (the honest count), `target.text:\n  \"*\"` and a `reason`: a focused password field, `mask: true`, or an\n  **unknown target** (the tree names no focused field; nothing is guessed, and no\n  other field is ever reported in its place). Masking only hides the echo: the\n  tree still shows what the app displays, so prove typing with the next\n  `acos_observe_tree`, not with the reply.","guide_all_topic":"all","guide_source":"docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md","guide_topic":"workflow","guide_topics":["overview","workflow","cli","scenarios","errors","limits","mcp"],"guide_unknown_topic":false,"loop":"inspect -> verify -> read verdict.json -> fix -> rerun","markdown":"# ACOS via MCP\n\nLoop: **inspect -> verify -> read verdict -> fix -> rerun**.\n\n## Currently available tools\n\n- `acos_inspect`\n- `acos_run_dex`\n- `acos_run`\n- `acos_observe`\n- `acos_action`\n- `acos_reset`\n- `acos_destroy`\n- `acos_verify`\n- `acos_capabilities`\n- `acos_help`\n- `acos_launch`\n- `acos_observe_tree`\n- `acos_tap`\n- `acos_key`\n- `acos_text`\n- `acos_swipe`\n- `acos_logs`\n- `acos_status`\n- `acos_doctor`\n\n## Not implemented yet (returns `unsupported`, never a pass)\n\n- `acos_capture` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\n- `acos_assert` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\n- `acos_events` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\n- `acos_profile` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\n- `acos_capability_report` — the acos backend does not implement this command yet\n\nCall `acos_help` with a topic for the full playbook, or `acos_capabilities` to re-probe.\n","supported":["acos_inspect","acos_run_dex","acos_run","acos_observe","acos_action","acos_reset","acos_destroy","acos_verify","acos_capabilities","acos_help","acos_launch","acos_observe_tree","acos_tap","acos_key","acos_text","acos_swipe","acos_logs","acos_status","acos_doctor"],"unsupported":[{"command":"capture","fidelity_when_available":"native-runtime","reason":"the acos backend does not implement this command yet","tool":"acos_capture"},{"command":"assert","fidelity_when_available":"native-runtime","reason":"the acos backend does not implement this command yet","tool":"acos_assert"},{"command":"events","fidelity_when_available":"native-runtime","reason":"the acos backend does not implement this command yet","tool":"acos_events"},{"command":"profile","fidelity_when_available":"native-runtime","reason":"the acos backend does not implement this command yet","tool":"acos_profile"},{"command":"capability-report","fidelity_when_available":"static-analysis","reason":"the acos backend does not implement this command yet","tool":"acos_capability_report"}],"workflow":["1. acos_inspect the APK (static only; never executes) to learn package, launcher, SDK, DEX/native inventory.","2. acos_capability_report (once implemented) or acos_capabilities to check the engine can do what the task needs.","3. acos_verify an APK + scenario into an artifacts directory; the verdict and step evidence are the product.","4. Read verdict.json and steps/NN-*.json on a non-pass; never guess.","5. Fix the app or the scenario, then rerun into a fresh directory. Reuse the same scenario so the comparison is meaningful.","6. A pass is valid only when its evidence exists; `unsupported` is never a pass."]},"status":"pass"}}}
